  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nToday through tomorrow night...\n\nThe latest satellite imagery reveals that the marine layer has \ncompletely inundated all of the coastal areas of both Orange and \nSan Diego counties, extending up to 10 miles inland for some \nportions of the inland areas, and even making its way far enough \neast for some areas of patchy fog in and around the I-15 corridor,\nso be advised and watch for quickly deteriorating visibilities if\nyou happen to be traveling through some of these areas of fog \nthis morning. Ceilings are generally hovering just under 1000 ft \nMSL for most locations , and a little lower from where they were \nyesterday. Otherwise, mostly clear skies will prevail and set up \nfor another very warm day with temperatures still running \ngenerally around 5 to 10 degrees above the average for this time \nof year. Winds aloft in the mid-levels have began to shift and \nwill become more southwesterly throughout today, drawing in drier \nair along with it. This will help to impede the chance of \nafternoon shower and thunderstorm development over the mountains. \nThe troughing upstream will also begin to influence the area with \nslightly lower heights that will begin a gradual cooling trend \nthat will continue through mid week. There will also begin the \npresence of a more predominant onshore flow, and this will likely \nhelp to draw in the marine layer a little sooner, and with better \ncoverage for tonight. In addition, this is also going to allow for\nwinds to increase through San Gorgonio Pass, and along the desert\nslopes, with gusts up around 40 mph through the early evening \nlater today. \n\nTuesday through Saturday...\n\nThe pattern remains rather stagnant through mid week, with the \nsubtle cooling and conditions remaining dry, and gusty winds in the \npasses during the afternoon. Thereafter, mid-level winds will begin \nto shift back to being from a more easterly direction, with better \npositioning of the high pressure center aloft to allow for some of \nthese monsoonal waves to develop and advect moisture over our \nregion. Temperatures will also start to gradually warm again during \nthis period, with increased humidity values and an increasing chance \nof getting thunderstorm activity for our mountains and deserts (and \npossibly even all the way to the coastal locations, depending on the \npropagation of these monsoonal waves as they develop over the \ndeserts to our southeast and then transition over our region. \nGuidance has also been showing an increase in moisture content and \nprecipitable water going into the beginning of the following \nweekend, which may further increase chances of developing \nthunderstorms during the afternoon over the mountains. During this\nperiod, the marine layer will likely struggle to develop, given \nthe increased mid to high level clouds moving overhead, in \naddition, with better offshore flow overnight.",
